{"collection":"hack","title":"Stop shelling out to basename, dirname, and sed: bash parameter expansion trims paths in-process","brief":"Every `$(basename \"$f\")` and `$(dirname \"$f\")` forks a subprocess; in a loop over 10,000 files that's 10,000 forks you never needed. Bash does it inline: `${f##*/}` is basename, `${f%/*}` is dirname, `${f%.*}` strips the extension, `${f##*.}` grabs it, and `${v,,}`/`${v^^}` lower/upper-case with no `tr`. Two failures stay in: `${f%.*}` on a name with no dot returns the whole string unchanged, so 'strip the extension' silently no-ops on `README`; and `,,`/`^^` are bash 4+ only, so the same script that runs on Linux dies on macOS's default bash 3.2 with a cryptic `bad substitution`. Every case is `echo`-testable before you trust it.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/0010/bash-scripting/","source_title":"Bash Scripting Fundamentals","rationale":"it-journey's bash-scripting quest teaches parameter expansion (${file%.*}, ${var,,}, ${file##*/}) straight; lifehacker's shell shelf covers brace expansion, set -euo pipefail, trap, readline shortcuts, redirection, heredocs, and type -a but never parameter expansion for path/case surgery. The subprocess-per-call waste, the silent no-op on a dotless name, and the bash-3.2 'bad substitution' break are real, offline-testable, and on-voice."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Stop faking a hash with two parallel arrays: bash associative arrays (declare -A) and the macOS gap","brief":"You need a lookup — env->URL, user->count — and reach for two indexed arrays you keep in lockstep, which drift the instant one gets sorted. Bash has a real map: `declare -A seen`, then `seen[$key]=$val`, read with `${seen[$key]}`, iterate keys with `${!seen[@]}`, count with `${#seen[@]}`. Three failures stay in: you MUST `declare -A` first — assign to a bare name and bash makes an ordinary INDEXED array where every non-numeric key silently evaluates to index 0, so the whole map collapses onto one entry; a key holding spaces or a glob character needs the quotes (`${seen[\"$k\"]}`) or it word-splits; and associative arrays are bash 4+, so macOS's default bash 3.2 rejects `declare -A` outright. Reproducible in a throwaway shell.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/0010/bash-scripting/","source_title":"Bash Scripting Fundamentals","rationale":"it-journey's bash-scripting quest introduces `declare -A` associative arrays; lifehacker has many shell hacks but nothing on associative arrays or the declare-A-first footgun. The 'forgot declare -A and every string key became index 0' collapse and the bash-3.2-on-macOS wall are famous, offline-testable, and on-voice — distinct from the parameter-expansion idea drawn from this same source."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Stop running your container as root: the USER line, and the bind mount it suddenly can't write","brief":"By default a container's process is root, so a break-in gets root INSIDE the container — and through a bind mount it writes files owned by root onto your host. Add a real user in the Dockerfile: `RUN adduser --disabled-password --gecos '' app` then `USER app`, and prove it with `docker run --rm img id`. The failures that stay in: flip to `USER app` and a later step that writes to a path root created dies with 'permission denied' (fix ownership with `COPY --chown=app:app` or a `chown` before you drop privileges); a non-root user cannot bind a port below 1024, so a process that wanted `:80` fails and you map a high port and let the host publish it; and a bind-mounted host directory keeps the HOST uid, so your in-container `app` (uid 1000) hits permission-denied on files your laptop user owns as a different uid. Reproducible with `docker run` and a mounted folder.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/0101/docker-mastery/","source_title":"Docker Containerization Mastery","rationale":"it-journey's docker-mastery quest teaches non-root containers via `adduser`/`USER` as a hardening step; lifehacker's Docker shelf covers the daemon error, layer caching, compose scaling, healthchecks, the v1/v2 rename, and the docker-group relogin but never dropping root with USER. The permission-denied-after-USER, the sub-1024-port block, and the bind-mount uid mismatch are real, reproducible, on-voice footguns nothing in the backlog touches."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Your Docker image ships the whole compiler: a multi-stage build and the COPY --from that leaves the toolchain behind","brief":"The image that BUILT your app drags the build tooling — compiler, dev headers, the node_modules you only needed to bundle — into production, so a 40 MB binary rides inside a 1.2 GB image. Split the Dockerfile: a `FROM golang AS build` stage that compiles, then a slim final stage (`FROM alpine` or distroless) whose only job is `COPY --from=build /out/app /app`. `docker image ls` shows the drop. The failures that stay in: `COPY --from=build` copies FILES, not installed system packages, so a binary that needs a shared library at runtime (a glibc build dropped onto Alpine's musl is the classic) built fine and then dies at startup with 'not found'; and referencing a stage you never named (forgot the `AS build`, or typo'd it) silently copies from a base image instead of erroring. Reproducible by building the same app one-stage vs multi-stage and comparing sizes.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/0101/docker-mastery/","source_title":"Docker Containerization Mastery","rationale":"it-journey's docker-mastery names multi-stage builds and 'shipping build tooling in the final image' as a core mistake; lifehacker's Docker hacks cover layer-cache ordering and .dockerignore bloat but never the multi-stage builder/final split. The 1.2 GB image, the COPY --from that leaves system libraries behind (Alpine musl vs glibc), and the unnamed-stage slip are real, reproducible, on-voice — distinct from the layer-caching idea already queued."}
{"collection":"hack","title":"Your generated file diffs on every run and nothing changed: sort the list and sort_keys the dump","brief":"You commit a machine-generated JSON/YAML artifact and every rebuild rewrites it — git blames you for a 'change' that's only key ordering and set-iteration jitter, and any cache keyed on its hash misses forever. Make the output a pure function of the input: sort the list before you serialize, pass `sort_keys=True` (Python `json.dumps`) so object keys land in a stable order, and never embed `datetime.now()` or an absolute path that varies per machine. Prove it by generating twice and `diff`-ing — byte-identical or it isn't deterministic. The failures that stay in: Python dicts preserve insertion order but `set` iteration does not, so one un-sorted set poisons the whole file; and float formatting drifts across versions, so round or format numbers explicitly when they have to match. This is the discipline a CI findings file lives or dies on.","voice":"how-to-practical","source_url":"https://it-journey.dev/quests/0100/self-operating-website-02-the-proving-grounds/","source_title":"The Self-Operating Website 02: The Proving Grounds","rationale":"The Proving Grounds teaches deterministic CI output — 'sort the findings and serialize with sort_keys=True so the same repo state produces a byte-identical findings.jsonl'; lifehacker IS a self-operating site whose meta docs describe its own harness's determinism but there's no general hack on making ANY generated file reproducible. The phantom-diff failure, the unsorted-set poison, and the timestamp/abs-path traps are real, `diff`-testable, and on-voice."}
